// ui::layout: the solver (plan section 2.4, "Solve"). `end_frame` calls `solve`, `solve` once more
// when a scroll region gained or lost a bar, and `position` alone when something only moved.
//
// The layout is integer arithmetic on device pixels. Every length of the tree is whole pixels by
// the time it gets here (tree.ctx converts each declaration once, when it is recorded), so there
// is nothing to snap afterwards, and sizes are worked out before any position is: the size of an
// element never depends on where it stands. Where a division leaves a remainder, the rules are:
//
//   grow      Spare room is shared in whole pixels: every grow child gets the same amount, and
//             the pixels left over go one each to the FIRST children. 100 pixels among three
//             empty grow children is 34, 33, 33.
//   shrink    Missing room is taken back in whole pixels: every grow child gives the same
//             amount, and the odd pixels are given up one each by the LAST children, so children
//             that started equal end as growing leaves them: three children of 40 in 100 pixels
//             are 34, 33, 33 again. The first children are never the smaller ones.
//   take      floor(room * fraction). The product is made in f32, so 0.7 of 100 is 70 although the
//             f32 nearest 0.7 is a little under it.
//   anchor    A placed child's and a root's corner is parent + floor(size * anchor) + offset.
//   centre    spare / 2 rounded down, for children in a box and for a text's lines alike: an odd
//             pixel of spare room is left after the content (right, or below). `end` is all of it.
//   min, max  Are pixels (converted by the length rule) and hold a size exactly. `min` wins when
//             it is larger than `max`.
//   text      A measured width is the sum of the advances rounded up; a line's height and a
//             text's size are whole pixels (text.ctx).
//
// How an element is sized along an axis (`ui::Size`):
//
//   fix    exactly its value.
//   FIT    its content plus its padding, held between `min` and `max`. A text's content is its
//          line (for wrapped text its longest paragraph); a box's is its flow children: their
//          sizes and the gaps between them added up along its axis, the largest of them across it.
//   GROW   starts as FIT. Along its parent's axis it then shares the parent's spare room with the
//          other grow children; when the parent is short it gives room up instead. Across the
//          parent's axis it fills the parent's inner size. Always held between `min` and `max`.
//   take   that fraction of the parent's inner size (less the gaps along the parent's axis), held
//          between `min` and `max`.
//
// The steps, in the order of the layout this one is modelled on (ZGF's `LayoutTree.Solve`):
//
//   1. Fit widths, last element to first, so children before parents.
//   2. Share widths, first element to last, so a parent's width is final before its children's:
//      `take` is resolved, a grow child across the axis fills, and along the axis spare room goes
//      to the grow children, or is taken back from them.
//   3. Text, at the widths now known: wrapped text is broken into lines, a single line marked
//      `Overflow::ellipsis` that does not fit is cut short and gets its dots.
//   4. Fit heights, then share heights, the same way. A wrapped text is as high as its lines.
//   5. Positions, first element to last: a root at its anchor on the surface; flow children one
//      after another along the parent's axis from its padding edge, `gap` apart, moved by the
//      aligned part of the spare room on each axis (spare is never negative, so children that do
//      not fit start at the start edge and run past the far one) and by the parent's scroll
//      offset; placed children at parent.xy + floor(parent.wh * anchor) + offset.
//
// A paint takes no part: it is drawn in its parent's coordinates. A placed child and a root add
// nothing to a parent's size and take none of its room.
//
// Where the plan says nothing, the rule is ZGF's unless a line here says otherwise:
//
//  - Sharing is of the spare room, not of the parent: every grow child gets the same amount on
//    top of what it was fitted to, so three empty grow children end up equal (but for the pixels
//    left over), and one that holds content (or has a `min`) ends up that much larger than the
//    others.
//  - A grow child stops at its `max`, and what it could not take is shared again among the
//    rest; a shrinking one stops at its `min`. Sharing goes in rounds: a round whose whole share
//    would take some children to their limit stops those there and moves nothing else, and the
//    round that stops none gives every child left the share and hands out the pixels left over
//    as above. So the children that did not stop all change by the same amount, to the pixel
//    (101 among a GROW with max 30 and a GROW is 30 and 71), and the rounds end when no room is
//    left or no child can change: at most one more than the parent has grow children.
//  - When grow children are all at their `min` and the parent is still short, nothing else
//    shrinks: the children overflow from the start edge. FIT, fix and take never give room up.
//  - A parent is fitted before any room is shared, so a FIT parent has no spare room: its grow
//    children along its axis keep their fitted sizes, and those across it fill it, which makes
//    them as large as the largest of their siblings.
//  - A `take` child counts as its `min` (0 unless set) when its parent is fitted, and then takes
//    its fraction of what the parent came to; in a FIT parent that makes the children overflow.
//    (ZGF counts it as 0 even when it has a `min`; with the default `min` the two are the same.)
//  - A placed child's `take` is a fraction of its parent's whole size, padding included, and a
//    placed GROW child fills that size on both axes. A root stands in the surface the same way.
//  - Text. Wrapped text whose width is FIT is sized as GROW, so it fills a column, and in a row
//    it takes the spare room or gives room up and wraps. A single line marked
//    `Overflow::ellipsis` whose width is FIT is sized as GROW with its own width as `max`: it
//    never grows past its text and gives room up when its parent is short. Any other width (fix,
//    take, GROW, and FIT for text that neither wraps nor has dots) is taken as declared. A line
//    is cut for its dots when it is wider than its room at all: both are whole pixels. Text that
//    does not wrap is one line whatever it holds.
//  - A text that finds the line table full is not laid out, or not past the lines that fitted,
//    and is counted once a frame however often the frame is solved.
//
// The solver cannot fail. It walks the flat array and never recurses; apart from the rounds of
// sharing its work is linear in the elements. Integer overflow would be a panic, so every sum is
// made in i64 and held before it is stored: a size is 0 to MAX_PIXELS, a position -MAX_PIXELS to
// MAX_PIXELS, whatever the program declared (a size that was not a number became 0 when it was
// recorded), and nothing is divided by zero.
//
// What a solve leaves in an element: `x`, `y`, `w`, `h`; for a box, `content_w` and `content_h`,
// the room its flow children take without its padding (their sizes and gaps along its axis, the
// largest of them across it) once every size is final, which is what a scroll region measures
// its range by; for a text, `first_line`, `line_count` and `dots`, with its lines in
// `ui.layout.lines` (byte ranges of the element's own text, and each line's width), and
// `content_h`, the height of those lines. A text's `content_w` stays the width of its one line.
// Emit reads `x`, `y`, `w`, `h` and a text's lines, and nothing else.
namespace ui {
    namespace layout {
        // The lines of every text element of the frame, in element order. `reported` is how many
        // texts this frame has counted as left without their lines.
        struct State { lines: []mut text::Line, line_count: u32, reported: u32 }

        fn make_state(S) { realloc: alloc::Fn(S), mut heap: S, cap: Capacity } -> !State {
            let lines = try alloc::make(text::Line, S){ realloc, &heap, count = @as(usize, cap.lines) }
            return State{ lines, line_count = 0, reported = 0 }
        }

        fn reset_state { mut ui: Ui } {
            ui.layout.line_count = 0
            ui.layout.reported = 0
        }

        // Solves the whole frame. It may run again on the same tree: every step starts from what
        // was recorded, not from what the last solve left.
        fn solve { mut ui: Ui } {
            fit_sizes{ &ui, wide = true }
            share_sizes{ &ui, wide = true }
            lay_text{ &ui }
            fit_sizes{ &ui, wide = false }
            share_sizes{ &ui, wide = false }
            position{ &ui }
        }

        // Whether a child takes room in its parent's flow.
        fn is_flow { e: tree::Element } -> bool {
            return e.kind != tree::Kind::paint and not e.placed and not e.root
        }

        // How much of `spare` pixels of room lies before content that is aligned so: none of it at
        // the start, half of it rounded down at the centre, all of it at the end. Room that is
        // missing (a negative spare) is none.
        fn align_spare { spare: i32, align: Align } -> i32 {
            if spare <= 0 { return 0 }
            match align {
                start => { return 0 }
                center => { return spare / 2 }
                end => { return spare }
            }
        }

        // `v` as a size: 0 when it is negative, and never more than MAX_PIXELS.
        fn hold_size { v: i64 } -> i32 {
            if v <= 0 { return 0 }
            return if v > 1000000000 { MAX_PIXELS } else { @as(i32, v) }
        }

        // `v` held between `lo` and `hi`, as a size. `lo` wins when it is larger than `hi`.
        fn clamp_size { v: i64, lo: i32, hi: i32 } -> i32 {
            let top = if hi < lo { lo } else { hi }
            if v > @as(i64, top) { return hold_size{ v = @as(i64, top) } }
            if v < @as(i64, lo) { return hold_size{ v = @as(i64, lo) } }
            return hold_size{ v }
        }

        // The padding of `e` along one axis, both sides together.
        fn sum_padding { e: tree::Element, wide: bool } -> i32 {
            return hold_size{ v = if wide { @as(i64, e.padding.left) + @as(i64, e.padding.right) } else { @as(i64, e.padding.top) + @as(i64, e.padding.bottom) } }
        }

        // How `e` is sized along one axis. It is what was declared, but for the width of a text
        // that was left FIT (the rules are at the top of the file): wrapped text grows, and a
        // single line with dots may give room up but never grows past its own width.
        fn get_size { e: tree::Element, wide: bool } -> tree::Extent {
            let size = if wide { e.width } else { e.height }
            if not wide or e.kind != tree::Kind::text or size.kind != SizeKind::fit { return size }
            if e.wrap { return tree::Extent{ kind = SizeKind::grow, min = size.min, max = size.max, .. } }
            if e.overflow == Overflow::ellipsis {
                let own = hold_size{ v = @as(i64, e.content_w) + @as(i64, sum_padding{ e, wide }) }
                return tree::Extent{ kind = SizeKind::grow, min = size.min, max = pick_max_i32{ a = size.min, b = pick_min_i32{ a = size.max, b = own } }, .. }
            }
            return size
        }

        // The size an element starts with, before its parent shares out any room: `content` is
        // its content with its padding.
        fn fit_size { size: tree::Extent, content: i64 } -> i32 {
            match size.kind {
                fixed => { return hold_size{ v = @as(i64, size.value) } }
                part => { return clamp_size{ v = 0, lo = size.min, hi = size.max } }
                else => { return clamp_size{ v = content, lo = size.min, hi = size.max } }
            }
        }

        // The size of an element once the room it stands in is known: a `take` its fraction of
        // `basis` rounded down, a GROW that is `filling` all of `basis`, anything else the size it
        // was fitted to.
        fn take_room { size: tree::Extent, fitted: i32, basis: i32, filling: bool } -> i32 {
            match size.kind {
                part => { return clamp_size{ v = @as(i64, take_fraction{ whole = basis, fraction = size.part }), lo = size.min, hi = size.max } }
                grow => {
                    if not filling { return fitted }
                    return clamp_size{ v = @as(i64, basis), lo = size.min, hi = size.max }
                }
                else => { return fitted }
            }
        }

        // What the flow children of one element take along an axis: their sizes added up, the
        // largest of them, and the gaps between them (negative when the gap is).
        struct Room { sum: i64, largest: i32, gaps: i64 }

        fn measure_children { ui: Ui, first: u32, gap: i32, wide: bool } -> Room {
            let mut out = Room{ sum = 0, largest = 0, gaps = 0 }
            let mut any = false
            let mut c = first
            while c != tree::NONE {
                let at = @as(usize, c)
                c = ui.tree.elements[at].next
                if not is_flow{ e = ui.tree.elements[at] } { continue }
                let size = if wide { ui.tree.elements[at].w } else { ui.tree.elements[at].h }
                out.sum = out.sum + @as(i64, size)
                out.largest = pick_max_i32{ a = out.largest, b = size }
                if any { out.gaps = out.gaps + @as(i64, gap) }
                any = true
            }
            return out
        }

        // The width a text asks for: its one line's, or for wrapped text that holds newlines the
        // width of its longest paragraph, which is as wide as wrapping can leave it.
        fn measure_natural { mut ui: Ui, e: tree::Element } -> i32 {
            if not e.wrap { return e.content_w }
            let text = get_text{ ui, span = e.text }
            let mut to: usize = 0
            while to < text.len and text[to] != '\n' { to = to + 1 }
            if to >= text.len { return e.content_w }
            let mut widest: i32 = 0
            let mut from: usize = 0
            while true {
                let width = text::measure_text{ cache = &ui.cache, list = ui.draw, provider = ui.provider, font = e.font, size = e.text_size, text = text[from..to] }
                widest = pick_max_i32{ a = widest, b = width }
                if to >= text.len { break }
                from = to + 1
                to = from
                while to < text.len and text[to] != '\n' { to = to + 1 }
            }
            return widest
        }

        // Steps 1 and 4, along one axis: widths when `wide`, otherwise heights. Every element
        // gets the size it has before any room is shared.
        fn fit_sizes { mut ui: Ui, wide: bool } {
            let mut i = @as(usize, ui.tree.count)
            while i > 0 {
                i = i - 1
                let e = ui.tree.elements[i]
                if e.kind == tree::Kind::paint { continue }
                let mut content = if wide { e.content_w } else { e.content_h }
                if e.kind == tree::Kind::box {
                    let room = measure_children{ ui, first = e.first_child, gap = e.gap, wide }
                    content = if (e.axis == Axis::row) == wide { hold_size{ v = room.sum + room.gaps } } else { room.largest }
                    if wide { ui.tree.elements[i].content_w = content } else { ui.tree.elements[i].content_h = content }
                } else if e.kind == tree::Kind::text and wide {
                    content = measure_natural{ &ui, e }
                }
                let size = fit_size{ size = get_size{ e, wide }, content = @as(i64, content) + @as(i64, sum_padding{ e, wide }) }
                if wide { ui.tree.elements[i].w = size } else { ui.tree.elements[i].h = size }
            }
        }

        // Whether a child can take spare room along its parent's axis (`grow`), or give room up.
        fn can_resize { e: tree::Element, wide: bool, grow: bool } -> bool {
            if not is_flow{ e } { return false }
            let size = get_size{ e, wide }
            if size.kind != SizeKind::grow { return false }
            let now = if wide { e.w } else { e.h }
            return if grow { now < size.max } else { now > size.min }
        }

        // How far a child that can change is from the limit that stops it: its `max` when it
        // grows, its `min` when it gives room up. At least 1 for a child `can_resize` allows.
        fn get_slack { e: tree::Element, wide: bool, grow: bool } -> i64 {
            let size = get_size{ e, wide }
            let now = @as(i64, if wide { e.w } else { e.h })
            return if grow { @as(i64, size.max) - now } else { now - @as(i64, size.min) }
        }

        // Gives `amount` pixels of room to the grow children in the list that starts at `first`,
        // or takes it from them, so that every child changes by the same amount or stops at its
        // limit. A round works out the whole share of what is left for each child that can still
        // change. If some of them are no further than a share from their limit, those stop there
        // and nothing else moves: what they could not take is shared again in the next round. When
        // none is, every child changes by the share, and the pixels the division left over go one
        // each to the first children (growing) or come one each from the last (shrinking), so the
        // children that did not stop end within a pixel of the same change, the larger ones first.
        // Every round but the last stops a child for good, so there is at most one round more
        // than there are grow children.
        fn resize_children { mut ui: Ui, first: u32, wide: bool, amount: i64, grow: bool } {
            let mut left = amount
            while left > 0 {
                let mut able: i64 = 0
                let mut c = first
                while c != tree::NONE {
                    let at = @as(usize, c)
                    c = ui.tree.elements[at].next
                    if can_resize{ e = ui.tree.elements[at], wide, grow } { able = able + 1 }
                }
                if able == 0 { return }
                let share = left / able

                // The children a whole share would take to their limit or past it stop there.
                let mut stopped = false
                c = first
                while c != tree::NONE {
                    let at = @as(usize, c)
                    c = ui.tree.elements[at].next
                    if not can_resize{ e = ui.tree.elements[at], wide, grow } { continue }
                    let slack = get_slack{ e = ui.tree.elements[at], wide, grow }
                    if slack > share { continue }
                    let size = get_size{ e = ui.tree.elements[at], wide }
                    let limit = if grow { size.max } else { size.min }
                    if wide { ui.tree.elements[at].w = limit } else { ui.tree.elements[at].h = limit }
                    left = left - slack
                    stopped = true
                }
                if stopped { continue }

                // Every child left is more than a share from its limit: they all change by it,
                // and by one pixel more for as many of them as the division left pixels over.
                let extra = left % able
                let mut k: i64 = 0          // which of the children that can change this one is
                c = first
                while c != tree::NONE {
                    let at = @as(usize, c)
                    c = ui.tree.elements[at].next
                    if not can_resize{ e = ui.tree.elements[at], wide, grow } { continue }
                    let odd = if grow { k < extra } else { k >= able - extra }
                    let change = if odd { share + 1 } else { share }
                    k = k + 1
                    let now = @as(i64, if wide { ui.tree.elements[at].w } else { ui.tree.elements[at].h })
                    let next = hold_size{ v = if grow { now + change } else { now - change } }
                    if wide { ui.tree.elements[at].w = next } else { ui.tree.elements[at].h = next }
                }
                return
            }
        }

        // Steps 2 and 4, along one axis: each element, whose own size is final by now, gives its
        // children theirs.
        fn share_sizes { mut ui: Ui, wide: bool } {
            let surface = if wide { ui.draw.width } else { ui.draw.height }
            for i in 0..@as(usize, ui.tree.count) {
                let e = ui.tree.elements[i]
                if e.kind == tree::Kind::paint { continue }
                let mut outer = if wide { e.w } else { e.h }
                if e.root {
                    // A root stands in the surface as a placed child stands in its parent.
                    outer = take_room{ size = get_size{ e, wide }, fitted = outer, basis = surface, filling = true }
                    if wide { ui.tree.elements[i].w = outer } else { ui.tree.elements[i].h = outer }
                }
                if e.first_child == tree::NONE { continue }

                let main = (e.axis == Axis::row) == wide
                let inner = hold_size{ v = @as(i64, outer) - @as(i64, sum_padding{ e, wide }) }
                let fitted = measure_children{ ui, first = e.first_child, gap = e.gap, wide }
                let room = if main { hold_size{ v = @as(i64, inner) - fitted.gaps } } else { inner }

                let mut used = fitted.gaps
                let mut c = e.first_child
                while c != tree::NONE {
                    let at = @as(usize, c)
                    c = ui.tree.elements[at].next
                    if ui.tree.elements[at].kind == tree::Kind::paint { continue }
                    let placed = ui.tree.elements[at].placed
                    let before = if wide { ui.tree.elements[at].w } else { ui.tree.elements[at].h }
                    let size = take_room{ size = get_size{ e = ui.tree.elements[at], wide }, fitted = before, basis = if placed { outer } else { room }, filling = placed or not main }
                    if wide { ui.tree.elements[at].w = size } else { ui.tree.elements[at].h = size }
                    if not placed { used = used + @as(i64, size) }
                }
                if main {
                    let spare = @as(i64, inner) - used
                    if spare > 0 {
                        resize_children{ &ui, first = e.first_child, wide, amount = spare, grow = true }
                    } else if spare < 0 {
                        resize_children{ &ui, first = e.first_child, wide, amount = 0 - spare, grow = false }
                    }
                }

                // The children's sizes along this axis are final now: what they take is the
                // element's content.
                let taken = measure_children{ ui, first = e.first_child, gap = e.gap, wide }
                let content = if main { hold_size{ v = taken.sum + taken.gaps } } else { taken.largest }
                if wide { ui.tree.elements[i].content_w = content } else { ui.tree.elements[i].content_h = content }
            }
        }

        // Step 3: the lines of every text element, at its width. Wrapped text is broken to its
        // inner width; any other text is one line, cut short for its dots when it is marked
        // `Overflow::ellipsis` and does not fit. A text is then as high as its lines.
        fn lay_text { mut ui: Ui } {
            ui.layout.line_count = 0
            let mut lost: u32 = 0
            for i in 0..@as(usize, ui.tree.count) {
                if ui.tree.elements[i].kind != tree::Kind::text { continue }
                let e = ui.tree.elements[i]
                let at = @as(usize, ui.layout.line_count)
                let inner = hold_size{ v = @as(i64, e.w) - @as(i64, sum_padding{ e, wide = true }) }
                let mut count: u32 = 0
                let mut dots = false
                if at >= ui.layout.lines.len {
                    lost = add_count{ a = lost, b = 1 }
                } else if e.wrap {
                    let text = get_text{ ui, span = e.text }
                    let wrapped = text::wrap_text{ cache = &ui.cache, list = ui.draw, provider = ui.provider, font = e.font, size = e.text_size, text, width = inner, lines = ui.layout.lines[at..] }
                    count = wrapped.lines
                    if @as(usize, wrapped.rest) < text.len { lost = add_count{ a = lost, b = 1 } }
                } else {
                    let mut line = text::Line{ start = 0, end = e.text.len, width = e.content_w }
                    if e.overflow == Overflow::ellipsis and e.content_w > inner {
                        let text = get_text{ ui, span = e.text }
                        let fit = text::fit_ellipsis{ cache = &ui.cache, list = ui.draw, provider = ui.provider, font = e.font, size = e.text_size, text, width = inner }
                        line = text::Line{ start = 0, end = fit.len, width = fit.width }
                        dots = fit.dots
                    }
                    ui.layout.lines[at] = line
                    count = 1
                }
                ui.tree.elements[i].first_line = ui.layout.line_count
                ui.tree.elements[i].line_count = count
                ui.tree.elements[i].dots = dots
                // A text that lost its lines keeps the height of one.
                ui.tree.elements[i].content_h = if count > 1 { hold_size{ v = @as(i64, e.line_height) * @as(i64, count) } } else { e.line_height }
                ui.layout.line_count = ui.layout.line_count + count
            }
            // Texts left without lines are dropped things: counted once, though a frame may be
            // solved twice.
            while ui.layout.reported < lost {
                report_drop{ &ui, kind = ProblemKind::lines_full }
                ui.layout.reported = ui.layout.reported + 1
            }
        }

        // Step 5. Sizes are final when it runs; it may run again after something moved. A
        // position is a sum of sizes, paddings, gaps, offsets and scroll, made in i64 and held.
        fn position { mut ui: Ui } {
            let mut i: usize = 0
            while i < @as(usize, ui.tree.count) {
                let mut e = ui.tree.elements[i]
                i = i + 1
                if e.kind == tree::Kind::paint { continue }
                if e.root {
                    e.x = add_pixels{ a = take_fraction{ whole = ui.draw.width, fraction = e.anchor_x }, b = e.offset_x }
                    e.y = add_pixels{ a = take_fraction{ whole = ui.draw.height, fraction = e.anchor_y }, b = e.offset_y }
                    ui.tree.elements[i - 1].x = e.x
                    ui.tree.elements[i - 1].y = e.y
                }
                if e.first_child == tree::NONE { continue }

                let row = e.axis == Axis::row
                let inner_w = hold_pixels{ v = @as(i64, e.w) - @as(i64, e.padding.left) - @as(i64, e.padding.right) }
                let inner_h = hold_pixels{ v = @as(i64, e.h) - @as(i64, e.padding.top) - @as(i64, e.padding.bottom) }
                let start_x = @as(i64, e.x) + @as(i64, e.padding.left) - @as(i64, e.scroll_x)
                let start_y = @as(i64, e.y) + @as(i64, e.padding.top) - @as(i64, e.scroll_y)

                let taken = measure_children{ ui, first = e.first_child, gap = e.gap, wide = row }
                let spare = hold_pixels{ v = @as(i64, if row { inner_w } else { inner_h }) - taken.sum - taken.gaps }
                let mut cursor = @as(i64, align_spare{ spare, align = if row { e.align_x } else { e.align_y } })
                let across = if row { e.align_y } else { e.align_x }

                let mut c = e.first_child
                while c != tree::NONE {
                    let at = @as(usize, c)
                    let child = ui.tree.elements[at]
                    c = child.next
                    if child.kind == tree::Kind::paint { continue }
                    if child.placed {
                        ui.tree.elements[at].x = hold_pixels{ v = @as(i64, e.x) + @as(i64, take_fraction{ whole = e.w, fraction = child.anchor_x }) + @as(i64, child.offset_x) }
                        ui.tree.elements[at].y = hold_pixels{ v = @as(i64, e.y) + @as(i64, take_fraction{ whole = e.h, fraction = child.anchor_y }) + @as(i64, child.offset_y) }
                    } else if row {
                        ui.tree.elements[at].x = hold_pixels{ v = start_x + cursor }
                        ui.tree.elements[at].y = hold_pixels{ v = start_y + @as(i64, align_spare{ spare = sub_pixels{ a = inner_h, b = child.h }, align = across }) }
                        cursor = cursor + @as(i64, child.w) + @as(i64, e.gap)
                    } else {
                        ui.tree.elements[at].y = hold_pixels{ v = start_y + cursor }
                        ui.tree.elements[at].x = hold_pixels{ v = start_x + @as(i64, align_spare{ spare = sub_pixels{ a = inner_w, b = child.w }, align = across }) }
                        cursor = cursor + @as(i64, child.h) + @as(i64, e.gap)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
